# Participant-level phenotype. NEVER COMMITTED.
#
# config/phenotype/<cohort>_phenotype.csv is age, sex and clinical scores for
# named subjects -- "who the person is", in the words of the README beside it.
# Cam-CAN's and CNeuroMod's data access agreements do not permit redistributing
# participant-level data, and this repository is pushed to GitHub.
#
# The BUILDER is tracked (tools/make_phenotype.py) and the OUTPUT is not: the
# recipe is the reproducible part, and anyone with their own data access can
# re-run it. Same split as `outputs/`.
#
# `config/*_participants.csv` stays tracked on purpose -- ids, task and
# exclusion reasons are facts about files, not about people.
config/phenotype/*.csv

# NOTHING BUT .ipynb BELONGS UNDER notebooks/ ANY MORE. These four are where
# data used to land before the reorganisation; the paths that wrote them now
# point elsewhere (see below), and these entries stay only so a leftover
# directory on an old checkout is not offered up as something to commit.
notebooks/cache/
notebooks/state_maps_data/
notebooks/state_maps_results/
# plotly dumps a copy of every figure here when a notebook calls fig.show()
# with the iframe renderer. Pure scratch.
notebooks/iframe_figures/
iframe_figures/

# Reference maps for the contextualisation arm (fetch_reference_maps.sh),
# ~285 MB. An INPUT, like an atlas -- nothing in the pipeline produces them --
# so they are not under outputs/. $FMRIDECOMP_REFMAPS moves it; this is the
# default. Not a dotted name: unlike a cache, this is a quarter of a gigabyte
# somebody fetched on purpose and will want to find.
reference_atlases/
.refmaps/

# Saved figures. A deliverable rather than data, hence a folder of its own
# rather than a place in the output tree.
figures/

# --------------------------------------------------- scratch and binaries ---
# Container IMAGES. Built or copied next to the repo for convenience; they are
# hundreds of MB and are rebuilt from the recipe, never from git.
#
# The RECIPES must be tracked -- they are the only record of what is inside an
# image. This used to ignore `containers/` wholesale, and stage45.def and
# README.md survived only because they entered the index BEFORE the rule
# existed (gitignore does not touch an already-tracked file). So a NEW
# definition was silently ignored by every `git add -A`: stage45_shap.def was
# written, reported as written, and never left the machine it was written on.
containers/*
!containers/*.def
!containers/README.md
*.sif

# Jupyter's default name for a notebook nobody has named yet.
notebooks/Untitled*.ipynb

# The contextualisation results moved into the output tree, which `outputs*`
# already covers. They include `subject_correlations.parquet` -- per-subject
# derived data under the same rule as everything else there: Cam-CAN and
# CNeuroMod do not permit redistributing participant-level data, and this
# repository is pushed.

# The HCP participants table. `config/*_participants.csv` is tracked on purpose
# for the cohorts whose (sub, task) mapping is a published constant, but this
# one is rows for named HCP subjects built from a local copy, and it sat
# untracked-and-unignored where a single `git add -A` would have committed it.
# The BUILDER is tracked (tools/make_participants.py); the output is not.
config/hcp7t_participants.csv
# Same rule for the 168-subject table of the hp2000_clean copy
# (config/hcp7t_fix.yaml). Named HCP subjects, built on the cluster.
config/hcp7t_fix_participants.csv
